// Closes the door this issue opened (#986): a fixed delay standing in for an
// observable post-condition is how a test suite becomes flaky one line at a time.
// 149 of them had accumulated under scripts/ before anyone counted.
//
// What this allows, and how to say so:
//
//   - A delay that IS the specification (a debounce, a banner's dwell time, a
//     retry backoff). Keep the `sleep`, write one line above it saying why that
//     number, and suppress with:
//       // biome-ignore lint/plugin: <the reason>
//   - Proving something does NOT happen. Use `neverHappens(label, fn, ms)` from
//     scripts/lib-wait.cts instead — it spends its whole window on purpose and the
//     name says as much, so no suppression is needed.
//
// Everything else has a post-condition worth naming: use `waitFor(label, fn)`.
//
// This rule can only see code Biome parses as JavaScript, which is why the harness
// evals are written as real functions passed to evalSource() rather than as
// template literals — a `sleep(60)` inside a template literal is invisible to it
// (measured on Biome 2.5.6).
//
// One root pattern per file, so the two shapes are branches of a single `or`.
language js

or {
  // The named helper.
  `$fn($ms)` where {
    $fn <: or {
      `sleep`,
      `wait`
    },
    $ms <: r"[0-9][0-9_]*",
    register_diagnostic(span=$ms, message="Fixed wait. Use waitFor(label, fn) for an observable post-condition, or neverHappens(label, fn, ms) to prove a non-event. If the delay itself is the spec, keep it with a one-line reason and `// biome-ignore lint/plugin: <reason>`.", severity="error")
  },
  // The same delay written inline. Matching only `sleep` would have left the idiom
  // it replaced wide open — `await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 400))` is what
  // every one of these files used before lib-wait.cts existed, and three harnesses
  // still did while this issue was being written.
  //
  // Deliberately narrow: it matches only an executor whose WHOLE body is a
  // setTimeout with a literal delay. A timeout guard — `new Promise((res, rej) => {
  // const t = setTimeout(() => rej(...), ms); ... })` — is a different thing and is
  // not reported (verified against both shapes).
  `new Promise($cb)` where {
    $cb <: r"\(?\s*\w+\s*\)?\s*=>\s*setTimeout\(\s*\w+\s*,\s*[0-9][0-9_]*\s*\)",
    register_diagnostic(span=$cb, message="Fixed wait written inline. Use sleep() from scripts/lib-wait.cts if the delay really is the spec (and say why with `// biome-ignore lint/plugin: <reason>`), otherwise waitFor(label, fn) for an observable post-condition.", severity="error")
  }
}
